{
  "about": "SHARED_IDENTITY_HOSTS: identity providers many stores and apps share. Native network blocking (iOS, Android) never runs on them, so it never refuses a request a shared sign-in page makes. This file is the source. Every SDK carries the same list and runs the vectors below in its tests. PROTOCOL.md, 'Native blocking hosts', has the same rule in prose.",
  "rule": [
    "Normalize a host: lowercase, and drop one trailing dot.",
    "A host is UNDER an entry E when it equals E or ends with '.' + E (a whole-label match: 'evilgoogle.com' is not under 'google.com').",
    "The store's NAMES: for each host in the store's hosts and authHosts (bundled, plus any signed widening) that is not under any entry, the first label of its registrable domain (registrableDomain in allowlist.ts: the last two labels, three for co.uk, com.au, co.nz). Safeway (safeway.com, albertsons.com, albertsons.okta.com) has the names safeway and albertsons; every Kroger banner has kroger.",
    "A host is EXCLUDED (shared) when it is under an entry E, unless it is a strict subdomain of E whose TENANT LABEL (the label just left of E) is one of the store's names. So okta.com and login.okta.com are excluded, albertsons.okta.com and www.albertsons.okta.com are not (for an Albertsons banner), kroger.okta.com is (for an Albertsons banner).",
    "A host that is under no entry is never excluded.",
    "The native blocking hosts of a store: its hosts, then its authHosts, in that order, without repeats, leaving out every excluded host. A request is refused natively only when its host equals or is a subdomain of a blocking host, its path matches BLOCKED_PATTERNS, and blocking is on."
  ],
  "hosts": [
